Title: Resolve TryAcquireSRWLock{Shared,Exclusive} at runtime for Vista
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

First blocker for Windows Vista. Electron reached the loader and died there:

  The procedure entry point TryAcquireSRWLockExclusive could not be located in
  the dynamic link library KERNEL32.dll

Both functions are Windows 7+. Vista shipped the SRW family -- InitializeSRWLock,
AcquireSRWLock{Shared,Exclusive} and their releases all exist there -- but not
the two non-blocking variants, and a static import of a symbol the DLL does not
export is fatal at load, before any of our code runs. That makes this a hard
gate rather than a bug you can reach and work around.

libuv already resolves SetThreadDescription and GetThreadDescription this way in
the same file, with a uv_once_t guard, a p-prefixed pointer and UV_ENOSYS when
the symbol is absent. This follows that pattern exactly rather than inventing a
second convention.

UV_ENOSYS rather than a fallback is deliberate. There is no way to ask a Vista
SRWLock for the lock without blocking, and returning 0 would tell a caller it
holds a lock it does not, corrupting whatever that lock guards. Reporting "this
platform cannot do that" is the honest answer, and it costs nothing here:
uv_rwlock_tryrdlock and uv_rwlock_trywrlock have no callers in Node or Electron.
The only ones in the tree are in libuv's own deps/uv/test/test-mutexes.c, which
is not part of this build -- verified by grep across electron_node.

Does not fix Vista on its own; it is the first symbol in the way.

diff --git a/deps/uv/src/win/thread.c b/deps/uv/src/win/thread.c
--- a/deps/uv/src/win/thread.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/win/thread.c
@@ -60,6 +60,9 @@
 static uv_once_t uv__thread_name_once = UV_ONCE_INIT;
 HRESULT (WINAPI *pGetThreadDescription)(HANDLE, PWSTR*);
 HRESULT (WINAPI *pSetThreadDescription)(HANDLE, PCWSTR);
+static uv_once_t uv__srwlock_try_once = UV_ONCE_INIT;
+BOOLEAN (WINAPI *pTryAcquireSRWLockShared)(PSRWLOCK);
+BOOLEAN (WINAPI *pTryAcquireSRWLockExclusive)(PSRWLOCK);
 
 
 static void uv__init_current_thread_key(void) {
@@ -426,8 +429,40 @@
 }
 
 
+/* TryAcquireSRWLockShared and TryAcquireSRWLockExclusive are Windows 7+. Vista
+ * has the rest of the SRW family but not these two, and a static import of a
+ * symbol kernel32 does not export stops the image loading at all -- which is
+ * exactly how Electron failed on Vista:
+ *
+ *   The procedure entry point TryAcquireSRWLockExclusive could not be located
+ *   in the dynamic link library KERNEL32.dll
+ *
+ * So resolve them the way this file already resolves SetThreadDescription, and
+ * report UV_ENOSYS where they are missing rather than faking success: a caller
+ * told it took the lock when it did not would corrupt whatever the lock guards.
+ * Nothing in Node or Electron calls uv_rwlock_tryrdlock/trywrlock -- libuv's
+ * own test suite is the only caller in the tree, and it is not built here -- so
+ * on Vista this trades an unreachable code path for a process that starts. */
+static void uv__srwlock_try_init_once(void) {
+  HMODULE m;
+
+  m = GetModuleHandleW(L"kernel32.dll");
+  if (m != NULL) {
+    pTryAcquireSRWLockShared = (void*)
+        GetProcAddress(m, "TryAcquireSRWLockShared");
+    pTryAcquireSRWLockExclusive = (void*)
+        GetProcAddress(m, "TryAcquireSRWLockExclusive");
+  }
+}
+
+
 int uv_rwlock_tryrdlock(uv_rwlock_t* rwlock) {
-  if (!TryAcquireSRWLockShared(&rwlock->read_write_lock_))
+  uv_once(&uv__srwlock_try_once, uv__srwlock_try_init_once);
+
+  if (pTryAcquireSRWLockShared == NULL)
+    return UV_ENOSYS;
+
+  if (!pTryAcquireSRWLockShared(&rwlock->read_write_lock_))
     return UV_EBUSY;
 
   return 0;
@@ -445,7 +480,12 @@
 
 
 int uv_rwlock_trywrlock(uv_rwlock_t* rwlock) {
-  if (!TryAcquireSRWLockExclusive(&rwlock->read_write_lock_))
+  uv_once(&uv__srwlock_try_once, uv__srwlock_try_init_once);
+
+  if (pTryAcquireSRWLockExclusive == NULL)
+    return UV_ENOSYS;
+
+  if (!pTryAcquireSRWLockExclusive(&rwlock->read_write_lock_))
     return UV_EBUSY;
 
   return 0;
